[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m Yes, I can provide an example of how to create a scatter plot with a regression line using the `statsmodels` library in Python. Suppose we have two arrays, `x` and `y`, representing the independent and dependent variables, respectively. We can use the `OLS` class from `statsmodels.api` to perform ordinary least squares regression and then plot the results using `matplotlib`.
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m ```python
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m import numpy as np
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m from statsmodels.api import OLS
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m # Seed the random number generator for reproducibility
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m np.random.seed(10)
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m # Generate random data points
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m x = np.linspace(0, 10, 50)
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m y = 3 * x + 5 + np.random.normal(0, 10, 50)
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m # Perform OLS regression
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m model = OLS(y, x)
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m results = model.fit()
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m # Plot the data points and the regression line
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m plt.scatter(x, y)
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m plt.plot(x, results.params[0] + results.params[1] * x, color='red')
